From: Y2Linux maintainers
Subject: [PATCH] Accept every file name a computer can send

Some music never copied over USB (Y2Linux #88). Three causes, all in the
name handling, none in the files:

- A name with ".." anywhere was refused as a path step ("05 I
  Still....m4a"). Only "." and ".." themselves are refused now: slashes
  and backslashes already become '_', so no name can leave its folder,
  and the platform's openat2 RESOLVE_BENEATH stays the real boundary.
- A character outside the Basic Multilingual Plane (most emoji) arrives
  as a UTF-16 surrogate pair. Each half became a 3-byte character of its
  own, invalid UTF-8, so the name was refused. The pair is one code point
  now, a lone half U+FFFD. The other direction ended a name at its first
  4-byte character, so a file copied by a card reader showed cut off on
  the computer: it is a surrogate pair now, never split at the 255-unit
  limit.
- ';' became '_'. FAT, exFAT, ext4 and Windows all allow it, and the
  renamed copy looked like a different file to sync tools (MusicBee
  copied it again on every sync). The characters FAT and exFAT forbid
  still become '_'.

A name of 255 UTF-16 units is up to 765 bytes of UTF-8: the name buffers
take 1024 bytes, so long CJK or emoji names are no longer cut.

diff --git a/inc/fs_handles_db.h b/inc/fs_handles_db.h
